{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 65097650dd1f495d85e0240ea6fb3feb173ae81e. All seventeen named artifacts examined; the lane report 902556bd is primary. The repaired measurements, retained scripts, readable zoom/pan policy, fit restoration, stacking and contributor-level transfer/retirement distinction address substantial parts of the earlier verdict. Three remaining issues prevent approval.\n\n1. Count units remain inconsistent (note sections 1–2, evidence section 3). At the pinned Gitseq depth 17533 I independently ran the unchanged UI projection: 925 lifecycle rows, 905 distinct requests/thread roots, 160 drawn roots, 758 hidden rows and 745 hidden roots. Therefore the displayed '925 tasks ... 745 tasks not shown' mixes units. The definition 'one request and its commitments' yields 905 tasks, not 925. Choose one honest user-facing unit and derive shown/hidden totals from row-to-drawn-root membership, including the 19 roots with repeated lifecycles; retain a test for that accounting. Chess likewise has 14 rows but 13 requests. Plain wording must not change the meaning of the measured number.\n\n2. Architecture and Simplification: layer 7's grouped provenance is being presented as task lineage. At pinned Chess depth 519, the #41 -\u003e #47 relation comes from later assertion #62 citing #41; the reverse includes README artifact #53 citing child promise #49/artifact #52. #47 \u003c-\u003e #54 similarly mixes review request/report citations with that later assertion. These are valid acyclic event citations grouped into thread cycles, not evidence that the tasks precede and follow each other. The evidence acknowledges grouping, but the product still tells the user '10 before, 9 after, 8 ... both ways' and requires visited-cycle navigation. Compare and recommend the smallest default showing recorded task/repair/review relationships, with supporting artifact/assertion citations available on selection. Define the exact existing evidence that permit